{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,280p' '/Users/smkzw/.cc-switch/skills/clinical-research/clinical-competitive-intelligence/SKILL.md'\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: clinical-competitive-intelligence\ndescription: 'Generate structured competitive intelligence reports for therapeutic area landscape analysis. Covers: competitor drug identification from registries/literature/regulatory sources, clinical trial design comparison, efficacy/safety data extraction, regulatory status tracking, drug lifecycle analysis, and clinical development strategy recommendations. Produces single-file interactive HTML reports with embedded SVG charts, structured SQLite databases, and evidence registries.\n\n  '\nmetadata:\n  triggers:\n  - competitive intelligence report\n  - 竞品调研\n  - 竞争格局分析\n  - clinical landscape analysis\n  - drug pipeline research\n  - 临床开发策略\n  - therapeutic area competitive analysis\n  - CFB/CFD/complement inhibitor landscape\n  - 研发进度调研\n  - ICH E1 safety database analysis\n  - 安全性数据库分析\n  - drug development safety database\n  - multi-indication development strategy\n  - 多适应症开发策略\n  - 安全数据库1500例\n---\n\n# Clinical Competitive Intelligence Report Generation\n\n## When to Use\n\nWhen the user requests a competitive landscape analysis, drug pipeline research, or clinical\ndevelopment strategy report for any therapeutic area. The workflow produces:\n\n1. **Structured SQLite database** with products, trials, efficacy, safety, regulatory, dev history\n2. **Evidence registry CSV** tracing every data point to its source\n3. **Single-file interactive HTML report** with SVG charts, filterable tables, and linked evidence\n\n## Workflow Overview\n\n### Phase 0: Workspace Initialization\n\n1. Create standardized directory structure:\n```\n<project_root>/\n├── 00_admin/          # task_state.json, master_plan, evidence_registry, QC files\n├── 01_raw/            # Downloaded raw files by source type\n│   ├── clinicaltrials_gov/\n│   ├── regulatory_fda/\n│   ├── regulatory_ema/\n│   ├── regulatory_nmpa_cde/\n│   ├── pubmed_pmc_journals/\n│   ├── company_news/\n│   ├── conferences/\n│   └── wechat_and_cn_media/\n├── 02_documents/      # Normalized documents (protocols, SAPs, publications, labels)\n├── 03_extracted/      # SQLite DB + CSV exports\n├── 04_figures_svg/    # SVG figures\n├── 05_report/         # Final HTML report\n├── 06_zotero_import/  # RIS/BibTeX + attachments\n└── 07_visual_review/  # Screenshots for QA\n```\n\n2. Scan existing files, build index with MD5 hashes, create `existing_files_index.csv`\n3. Initialize SQLite schema (see `references/sqlite-schema.md`)\n4. Create `task_state.json` tracking progress per phase\n\n### Phase 1: Competitor Drug & Trial Identification\n\n**Search ClinicalTrials.gov** with multiple query strategies:\n- Disease name + synonyms (e.g., \"paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria\", \"PNH\", \"hemoglobinuria, paroxysmal\")\n- Target names (e.g., C5, C3, CFB, CFD, MASP-3, complement)\n- Known drug names, brand names, development codes\n- Company/sponsor names\n\n**Key pitfall**: Don't rely only on the Condition field. Some studies use title/description\nfor PNH indication. Search broadly, then filter with explicit inclusion/exclusion criteria.\n\n**For each NCT record**, extract: study design, phase, status, arms, sample size, eligibility\ncriteria (Hb threshold, LDH threshold, transfusion requirement, prior treatment, washout),\nprimary/secondary endpoints, AESI, extension/rollover design.\n\n**Cross-reference** with regional registries (chinadrugtrials.org.cn, EU CTR) where accessible.\nMark \"not found\" when a registry is inaccessible — never fabricate CTR/regional IDs.\n\n### Phase 2: Evidence Collection (Parallel Subagents)\n\nUse `delegate_task` with 3-5 parallel subagents for independent research streams:\n- **Agent A**: Registry + trial design extraction\n- **Agent B**: Protocol/SAP PDF search and download\n- **Agent C**: Publication search (PubMed, PMC, conference abstracts)\n- **Agent D**: Regulatory documents (FDA Drugs@FDA, EMA EPAR, NMPA/CDE)\n- **Agent E**: Company sources, news, Chinese media\n\nEach subagent returns: structured findings with URLs, dates, source reliability level.\nMain agent merges results — subagents write to temp files, main agent consolidates.\n\n**Evidence hierarchy**: See `references/evidence-hierarchy.md`\n\n### Phase 3: Data Extraction & Database Population\n\nExtract from collected sources into SQLite tables:\n- `products`: Drug master data (target, mechanism, modality, route, regulatory status)\n- `trials`: Trial design (NCT, phase, arms, eligibility, endpoints)\n- `efficacy`: Efficacy results by trial/arm/endpoint (long format)\n- `safety`: Safety events by trial/arm/event (long format)\n- `baseline`: Baseline characteristics (demographics, disease, labs)\n- `regulatory_status`: FDA/EMA/NMPA approval status\n- `development_history`: Full lifecycle timeline\n- `publications`: Paper/trial/publication mapping\n\n**Key pitfall**: ClinicalTrials.gov auto-extracted results often have empty `drug_name` fields\nin efficacy/safety tables. Build a `trial_uid → drug_name` mapping from the trials table\nor trial_arms table before aggregating.\n\n### Phase 4: HTML Report Generation\n\nBuild single-file interactive HTML. See `references/html-report-template.md` for full template.\n\n**Structure**: Cover → Chapter 1 (Trial Design) → Chapter 2 (Results) → Chapter 3 (Lifecycle) → Chapter 4 (Strategy) → Appendix\n\n**Critical pattern**: Generate HTML in chunks using `execute_code` + `write_file` to avoid\ncontext overflow. Write CSS/head first, then each chapter, then JS, then assemble.\n\n**Charts**: All charts must be inline SVG (no Canvas, no external CDN, no bitmap charts).\nGenerate SVG strings in Python, embed directly in HTML.\n\n**Tables**: Use `overflow-x:auto` wrapper for wide tables. Filter bars with JS for\ninteractive filtering. Sticky headers.\n\n**Color scheme**: Primary accent `#FF9900`, info `#2196F3`, success `#4CAF50`,\ndanger `#F44336`. Medical/professional aesthetic — dense information, minimal decoration.\n\n### Phase 5: Quality Assurance\n\n1. Open in browser, verify at 1440px and 1920px\n2. Check: navigation works, SVGs render, tables scroll, links clickable, no text overflow\n3. Independent medical review: evidence gaps, misleading comparisons, unit inconsistencies\n4. Save screenshots to `07_visual_review/`\n5. Fix issues, regenerate, version increment\n\n## File Naming Conventions\n\n**Protocols**: `【靶点】【药物】【申办方】【分期】【年份】【关键III期与否】【NCT编号】【监管状态】研究方案.pdf`\n**Publications**: `【靶点】【药物】【申办方】【分期】【发表年份】【NCT编号】【监管状态】结果.pdf`\n**FDA docs**: `【靶点】【药物】【申办方】【适应症】-FDA说明书.pdf`\n\nSanitize illegal characters: `/ : ? * \" < > |` → replace with `_`\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n1. **ClinicalTrials.gov drug_name mapping**: Efficacy/safety result tables extracted from CT.gov\n   often have empty drug_name. Always build NCT→drug mapping from trials/trial_arms tables first.\n\n2. **Cross-trial comparison**: Different trials use different timepoints (12/16/24/26 weeks),\n   different Hb thresholds, different LDH normalization criteria. Always footnote these\n   differences — never present a bare comparison table without context.\n\n3. **Hb unit conversion**: Record both original unit and g/L. 1 g/dL = 10 g/L.\n\n4. **Small sample caution**: Phase 1/2 trials with n<30 need explicit sample size annotation\n   on all charts and tables. Don't rank small-n results alongside large-n Phase 3 data.\n\n5. **Chinese media verification**: WeChat/公众号 articles (except 丁香园 Insight, 医药魔方,\n   药智网) must be verified against official/primary sources before entering the database.\n\n6. **HTML chunk assembly**: When generating large HTML, write to temp files per section, then\n   assemble. Don't try to build 100KB+ HTML in a single string variable — context overflow risk.\n\n7. **SVG text rotation**: Drug name labels on matrix charts need `transform=\"rotate(-45,x,y)\"`\n   to avoid overlap. Test with actual drug name lengths.\n\n8. **Regulatory status disambiguation**: Same drug may have multiple application numbers,\n   indications, and label versions. Filter to the target indication before recording status.\n\n9. **Version numbering**: Check existing files in the project directory before assigning version.\n   Auto-increment if same-date same-version exists. Run `ls -la <dir>/*V0.*.html` before writing\n   to avoid overwriting a prior version generated in a different session.\n\n10. **ICH E1 exposure**: Don't cite ICH E1 from memory. See `references/ich-e1a-guideline-full-text.md`\n    for exact wording and `references/ich-e1-safety-database-analysis.md` for the full regulatory\n    analysis. Critical nuance: the 1500-patient threshold is an \"anticipated... about\" number, not\n    a hard requirement. The guideline itself lists exceptions (§7) including §7(e) — \"a smaller\n    number of patients may be acceptable... where the intended treatment population is small\" —\n    which is the primary basis for rare disease approvals with <200 patients. Always distinguish\n    between common disease targets (where 1500+ is expected) and rare disease drugs (where §7(e)\n    applies and smaller databases are explicitly allowed).\n\n11. **HTML report code audit checklist** (V0.8 lesson — 3 bugs found): Before delivering a large\n    single-file HTML report, run these automated checks via `grep` + browser console:\n    - **Undefined JS functions**: Search for function names in `onclick`/`onchange` handlers that\n      are never defined in `<script>`. E.g. `filterTable` called in sidebar but missing from JS.\n    - **Empty container divs**: Check `getElementById` targets — any `<div id=\"table-X\"></div>` or\n      `<div id=\"chart-X\"></div>` that `renderAllCharts()` never populates leaves a blank section.\n    - **Drug name typos**: `grep` for known drug names and check for near-misses (e.g. raculizumab\n      vs ravulizumab). Drug names appear in many contexts and typos propagate silently.\n    - **Browser console validation**: After opening the report, run in console:\n      `JSON.stringify({pages: [...document.querySelectorAll('.page')].map(p=>p.id+':'+(p.classList.contains('active')?'visible':'hidden')), charts: [...document.querySelectorAll('[id^=chart-]')].map(e=>e.id+':'+(e.innerHTML.length>10?'OK':'EMPTY')), tables: [...document.querySelectorAll('[id^=table-]')].map(e=>e.id+':'+(e.querySelector('table')?'OK':'MISSING'))})` \n      to verify all containers rendered.\n\n12. **Fact verification for recently-approved drugs**: When reviewing a report's regulatory claims\n    for drugs with very recent approval dates (within days), cross-verify against NMPA/FDA/EMA\n    official announcements via web search. Approval dates reported in Chinese media may differ\n    from official announcement dates by 1-2 days. Use the official date, not media date.\n\n13. **ICH E1 safety database (multi-indication drugs)**: See `references/ich-e1-safety-database-analysis.md`\n    for the complete workflow on how to research and present how a drug achieves the ICH E1\n    1500-patient safety database threshold through multi-indication development. Key technique:\n    extract total exposed from FDA label Section 6.1 first, then reconstruct per-indication\n    contribution from ClinicalTrials.gov v2 API, then build cumulative timeline per regulatory\n    milestone. See reference for common strategies (parallel vs sequential vs basket).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
